WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.700
Sandboxing is also a reliability control.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:15.880
A worker that handles complex input should not be able to consume unlimited CPU, memory, disk, handles, queue space, child processes, or wall-clock time.

3
00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:24.620
Resource limits give the broker a way to keep the rest of the application responsive when worker behavior is slow, expensive, or invalid.

4
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:30.720
Limits should be measurable, documented, and tuned from realistic workloads instead of guesses.

5
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.600
Timeouts and cancellation should be part of the protocol.

6
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:43.180
A broker may need to cancel a job when the user closes a document, when a worker exceeds a deadline, when shutdown begins, or when backpressure reaches a limit.

7
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:51.760
Cancellation should preserve state: partial output is cleaned up, temporary files are removed or marked incomplete, and broker-owned resources are released.

8
00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:57.380
The user experience should communicate recoverable failure without exposing internal details.

9
00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.460
Crash and restart behavior should be predictable.

10
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:06.480
A worker can exit unexpectedly, fail to launch, return partial results, or stop responding.

11
00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:15.460
The broker should detect those cases, record safe diagnostics, decide whether to restart, and avoid treating incomplete results as trusted output.

12
00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:18.680
Restart policy should avoid uncontrolled loops.

13
00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:23.180
Recovery should protect user data before trying to resume work or retrying a job.

14
00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:26.340
The broker's invariants matter most.

15
00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:35.480
It should not leak sensitive data, keep stale capabilities alive, lose track of files, or leave privileged operations half-complete because a worker failed.

16
00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:45.580
Tests should cover stuck jobs, repeated worker failure, cancellation during IPC, oversized input, denied permissions, and shutdown while work is pending.

17
00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:53.360
Monitoring should show whether failures are rare, concentrated in one worker, or linked to a recent feature or policy change.

18
00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:57.540
That evidence helps teams improve reliability without weakening the boundary.
